          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a note says \"use additional code\", is that optional?",
          "answer": "\"Use additional code\" is not optional. It tells you to report a second code alongside the main one.\n\nThe source is the educational summary \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.\" It says that \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" appear as a pair. They fix the order of reporting: the underlying condition is reported first, and its manifestation follows. \"Code also\" is a looser signal that two codes are needed, without setting an order.\n\nThe coding guide for type 2 diabetes (\"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories\") gives an example. Some diabetes codes still need a second code, such as a stage code for diabetic kidney disease or a site-and-depth code for a diabetic foot ulcer. The combination code names the link, and the additional code adds detail.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not say what happens if a coder ignores such a note in practice, or how a particular payer or audit treats it. They also do not cover the full list of notes in the classification. If you are working on a specific record, the official ICD-10-CM tabular list and a qualified medical coder or your organisation's coding policy are the right sources to confirm the exact instruction.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
